window.__labLoaded({"id":"4.2","title":"Standard Error","goal":"Compute the standard error of a mean by formula, then check it against simulated sample means and watch it shrink as n grows.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"SE by formula","text":"The lesson's eight midterm scores. The standard error is the sample SD (ddof=1) divided by the square root of n. It says how precisely this class mean is pinned down.","code":"import numpy as np\n\nscores = np.array([72, 85, 68, 91, 77, 83, 64, 80])\nn = len(scores)\nmean = scores.mean()\ns = scores.std(ddof=1)\nse = s / np.sqrt(n)\n\nprint(f'mean = {mean:.2f}')\nprint(f's    = {s:.2f}')\nprint(f'SE   = {se:.2f}')","out":"","figs":[]},{"title":"A population to test it on","text":"To check the formula we need many samples, so we build a skewed population (true mean 30, true SD 15) and draw a lot of samples from it.","code":"rng = np.random.default_rng(7)\npopulation = 15 + rng.exponential(15, size=100_000)\nsigma = population.std()\nprint(f'population SD = {sigma:.2f}')","out":"","figs":[]},{"title":"SE shrinks as n grows","text":"For each n we draw 2,000 samples and take the SD of their means. That should match sigma / sqrt(n), and also the SE we would get from a single sample. Each time n is multiplied by 4, the SE halves.","code":"print('   n   SD of 2000 means   sigma/sqrt(n)   s/sqrt(n), one sample')\nfor n in (10, 40, 160, 640):\n    means = rng.choice(population, size=(2000, n)).mean(axis=1)\n    one = rng.choice(population, size=n)\n    print(f'{n:4d}   {means.std(ddof=1):14.2f}   {sigma / np.sqrt(n):13.2f}   {one.std(ddof=1) / np.sqrt(n):12.2f}')","out":"","figs":[]},{"title":"Plan a sample size","text":"To get an SE of 1 when sigma is about 15, solve 15 / sqrt(n) = 1. Always round the answer up.","code":"target_se = 1\nsigma_guess = 15\nn_needed = int(np.ceil((sigma_guess / target_se) ** 2))\nprint('sample size needed:', n_needed)","out":"","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"scores <- c(72, 85, 68, 91, 77, 83, 64, 80)\nmean(scores)\nsd(scores)\nsd(scores) / sqrt(length(scores))\nset.seed(7)\nfor (n in c(10, 40, 160, 640)) print(sd(replicate(2000, mean(15 + rexp(n, 1/15)))))","expect":"mean 77.5; sd 9.087; standard error 3.213; then four simulated SDs near 4.7, 2.35, 1.18 and 0.59 (Python: 4.79, 2.36, 1.20, 0.57)","note":"sd() already uses n - 1, like ddof=1. The simulated SDs are close to, not identical to, Python's; the formula SE of 3.21 is exact."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Descriptives > Options > S.E. mean","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / score.\nBEGIN DATA\n72 85 68 91 77 83 64 80\nEND DATA.\nDESCRIPTIVES VARIABLES=score\n  /STATISTICS=MEAN SEMEAN STDDEV.","note":"Read the mean, the Std. Error of the mean (3.21) and the Std. Deviation (9.09) in the table. Simulations are possible but are not needed for this lesson."},"stata":{"code":"clear\ninput score\n72\n85\n68\n91\n77\n83\n64\n80\nend\nci means score\nsummarize score\ndisplay r(sd) / sqrt(r(N))","note":"ci means prints the mean and its Std. err. (3.21); the last line recomputes it as s divided by the square root of n."},"try":["Add a ninth score of 40 and predict whether the SE goes up or down, then run it.","In the simulation loop, change the sizes to (25, 100, 400) and check the SE halves each time.","Change sigma_guess to 30 and predict the sample size needed for an SE of 1."]});
